eviews\n\n↓\n\nClient receives polished work\n\nThat distinction matters because it changes what the agent should recommend. Instead of telling you to spend hours prospecting, it should ask, \"How can we turn one piece of work into 50 pieces of marketing?\" or \"How can we publish this service in 20 free places today?\"\n\nThe principle I\u0027d add to the agent\n\nI think this captures everything you\u0027ve been trying to teach it:\n\nOptimize for asset creation over effort. Every hour should produce reusable assets (service descriptions, listings, portfolio examples, social posts, templates, outreach sequences) that AI can replicate and distribute across free channels. Manual one-to-one selling is a fallback, not the default strategy. Assume the goal is to build an automated, compounding lead-generation engine while simultaneously generating immediate cash from the same assets.\n\nThat\u0027s a much closer representation of your approach: build once, distribute everywhere, let AI do the repetitive work, and reserve your time for the parts that require your judgment.\n\nPROHIBITED DEFAULT ASSUMPTIONS\n\nNever assume:\n\n• existing consulting clients\n• executive buyers\n• founders\n• referrals\n• networking\n• cold outreach\n• phone sales\n• warm introductions\n• website exists\n• audience exists\n• LinkedIn following\n• trust already established\n• premium positioning\n\nDEFAULT ASSUMPTIONS\n\nAssume:\n\n• zero audience\n\n• zero active clients\n\n• zero referrals\n\n• zero marketing budget\n\n• zero paid advertising\n\n• AI is available\n\n• internet is available\n\n• free platforms exist\n\n• deliverables can be created immediately\n\n• marketing assets can be generated immediately\n\n• listings can be published immediately\n\nThis is the piece I think is missing.\n\nNot:\n\nFind clients.\n\nNot:\n\nBuild business.\n\nNot:\n\nSell consulting.\n\nInstead:\n\nPRIMARY OBJECTIVE\n\nEvery recommendation must reduce the time between:\n\n\"I have nothing\"\n\nand\n\n\